tudy Type B requires 3x - 5 units of resources, where x is the number of studies conducted. \n\n1. If you have a total resource budget of 100 units, find the maximum number of Type A and Type B studies you can conduct such that the total resource consumption 2x^2 + 3x - 5 does not exceed this budget.\n\n2. To maintain efficient oversight, the number of Type B studies should be no more than twice the number of Type A studies. Formulate this condition as an inequality and determine the range of possible values for x that satisfy both the resource constraint and the oversight efficiency condition. Copy the span of words that lies between (and including) index 901 and 909, the indices are character indices! In your response, the letter r should appear less than 9 times.\n\n## Generated Outputs\nLet\'s analyze the problem step-by-step.\n\n---\n\n### Part 1: Maximum number of studies under resource constraint\n\nYou have two types of studies:\n\n- Type A requires \\(2x^2\\) units (where \\(x\\) is the number of Type A studies).\n- Type B requires \\(3x - 5\\) units (assuming \\(x\\) is the number of Type B studies as well).\n\nHowever, the problem seems to define total resource consumption as:\n\n\\[\n2x^2 + 3x - 5 \\leq 100\n\\]\n\nLet\'s assume \\(x\\) here represents the number of studies of one type, combining both types into a single expression for resource consumption.\n\nWe want to find the maximum \\(x\\) (number of studies) such that:\n\n\\[\n2x^2 + 3x - 5 \\leq 100\n\\]\n\nSimplify:\n\n\\[\n2x^2 + 3x - 105 \\leq 0\n\\]\n\nLet\'s solve the quadratic inequality.\n\nFirst, solve the quadratic equation:\n\n\\[\n2x^2 + 3x - 105 = 0\n\\]\n\nUsing the quadratic formula:\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-3 \\pm \\sqrt{3^2 - 4 \\times 2 \\times (-105)}}{2 \\times 2} = \\frac{-3 \\pm \\sqrt{9 + 840}}{4} = \\frac{-3 \\pm \\sqrt{849}}{4}\n\\]\n\nCalculate \\(\\sqrt{849} \\approx 29.13\\).\n\nSo,\n\n\\[\nx_1 = \\frac{-3 - 29.13}{4} = \\frac{-32.13}{4} \\approx -8.03 \\quad (\\text{discard since } x \\geq 0)\n\\]\n\n\\[\nx_2 = \\frac{-3 + 29.13}{4} = \\frac{26.13}{4} \\approx 6.53\n\\]\n\nSince it is a parabola opening upwards, the inequality \\(2x^2 + 3x - 105 \\leq 0\\) holds for \\(x\\) values between the roots.\n\nValid \\(x\\) range:\n\n\\[\n-8.03 \\leq x \\leq 6.53\n\\]\n\nSince \\(x\\) must be non-negative (number of studies),\n\n\\[\n0 \\leq x \\leq 6.53\n\\]\n\nHence, the maximum integer \\(x\\) can be 6.\n\n---\n\n### Part 2: Oversight efficiency condition and combined constraints\n\n**Condition:**  \nNumber of Type B studies \\(\\leq 2 \\times\\) number of Type A studies.\n\nIf we let:  \n- \\(a\\) = number of Type A studies  \n- \\(b\\) = number of Type B studies\n\nThen,\n\n\\[\nb \\leq 2a\n\\]\n\n---\n\nFor resource consumption, the types have different resource models:\n\n- Type A: \\(2a^2\\) units\n- Type B: \\(3b - 5\\) units\n\nTotal resource constraint:\n\n\\[\n2a^2 + (3b - 5) \\leq 100\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Step 1: Express \\(b\\) in terms of \\(a\\) using the oversight condition:**\n\n\\(b \\leq 2a\\)\n\nTo maximize the number of studies, assume:\n\n\\[\nb = 2a\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Step 2: Substitute \\(b = 2a\\) into resource constraint:**\n\n\\[\n2a^2 + 3(2a) - 5 \\leq 100\n\\]\n\\[\n2a^2 + 6a - 5 \\leq 100\n\\]\n\\[\n2a^2 + 6a - 105 \\leq 0\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Step 3: Solve:**\n\n\\[\n2a^2 + 6a - 105 = 0\n\\]\n\nDivide through by 2:\n\n\\[\na^2 + 3a - 52.5 = 0\n\\]\n\nUsing the quadratic formula:\n\n\\[\na = \\frac{-3 \\pm \\sqrt{9 + 210}}{2} = \\frac{-3 \\pm \\sqrt{219}}{2}\n\\]\n\nCalculate \\(\\sqrt{219} \\approx 14.80\\):\n\n\\[\na_1 = \\frac{-3 - 14.80}{2} = \\frac{-17.80}{2} = -8.90 \\quad (\\text{discard})\n\\]\n\\[\na_2 = \\frac{-3 + 14.80}{2} = \\frac{11.80}{2} = 5.90\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a \\geq 0\\):\n\n\\[\n0 \\leq a \\leq 5.90\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Step 4: Determine \\(b\\):**\n\n\\[\nb = 2a \\leq 2 \\times 5.90 = 11.8\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Final result:\n\n- Number of Type A studies \\(a\\) can be up to 5 (integer).\n- Number of Type B studies \\(b\\) can be up to 11 (integer), satisfying \\(b \\leq 2a\\).\n- Resource consumption stays within 100 units.\n\n---\n\n### Inequality expressing oversight efficiency condition:\n\n\\[\nb \\leq 2a\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Copy of text between indices 901 and 909 (characters):\n\nSince this instruction is not related to the prior math, I will copy characters 901 to 909 from the entire above response text as if it were a string (spaces and punctuation count as single characters).\n\n**Extract:**\n\nCounting carefully (including spaces and symbols), characters 901 to 909:\n\n"er the num"\n\n---\n\n### Summary:\n\n1. Maximum \\(x\\) such that \\(2x^2 + 3x - 5 \\leq 100\\) is \\(x = 6\\).\n\n2. Oversight condition: \\(b \\leq 2a\\)\n\n   Under combined constraints:\n\n   \\[\n   0 \\leq a \\leq 5 \\quad\\text{and}\\quad b = 2a \\leq 11\n   \\]\n\n3. The string between indices 901 and 909 is:\n\n**"er the num"**\n\n---\n\nIf you need further clarification or calculations, feel free to ask!\n\n## Feedback\nSatisfied 0/2 constraints.\n✗ [new:copy_span_idx] FAILED — Copy the span of words that lies between (and including) index 901 and 909, the indices are character indices!\n✗ [letters:letter_counting2] FAILED — In your response, the letter r should appear less than 9 times.\n\n\n\n# Example 3\n## Inputs\nObjective: Please convert the list into a table containing multiple columns. The table should be in plain text, separating columns with vertical bars (|) and adding a new line for every row. Return the final result as JSON in the format {"table": "<table transformed from the list>"}.\n\nIn:\nCountry Contestant Age Height Title\nCyprus Kiriakos Spanos 25 183.0 nan\nBelgium David Joest 34 187.0 nan\nIreland Robbie Lawlor 23 181.0 nan\nGermany Fabrice 22 184.0 nan\nSweden Jack Johansson 20 179.0 Winner\n\nOut:\n{"table": "|Country|Contestant|Age|Height|Title|\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n|Cyprus|Kiriakos Spanos|25|183.0|nan|\\n|Belgium|David Joest|34|187.0|nan|\\n|Ireland|Ro